---
// Phase 1.2/1.5 verification page. Lives at src/pages/dev/ (no underscore —
// see ADR-0014). Not a real site page; excluded from what ships via the
// deploy-time `rm -rf dist/dev` step (IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md §10), not via
// Astro's routing. Delete once the real specimen page (Phase 1.7) supersedes it.
import '../../styles/global.css';

// `?raw` pulls the file's literal source text at build time, as a string —
// no execution, no transformation. Combined with `is:inline` + `set:html`
// below, this guarantees theme-init.js reaches the page byte-for-byte,
// exactly as the file on disk reads. Astro must NOT be allowed to touch
// this script (no bundling, no `type="module"`, see the 1.5 write-up for
// why that specifically breaks the no-flash guarantee) while still keeping
// ONE real, unit-tested source file rather than a duplicated inline copy.
import themeInitSource from '../../lib/theme-init.js?raw';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <!-- Must run before anything else in <head> that could cause a paint,
         and must be genuinely inline (see import comment above). This is
         the entire no-flash guarantee, in one script tag. -->
    <script is:inline set:html={themeInitSource} />
    <style>
      /* Everything below reads from the real tokens (var(--c-*)) instead of
         hardcoded hex. This was the actual bug: the first version of this
         page used #fff/#666/#eee directly, so toggling data-theme changed
         nothing visible — there was nothing on the page listening to the
         theme at all. It's also almost certainly why the page looked overly
         bright: it forced a stark white background regardless of theme or
         the reader's OS preference. */
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 2rem;
        background: var(--c-bg);
        color: var(--c-text);
      }
      .row {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        gap: 2rem;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
      }
      .sample {
        font-size: 24px;
        line-height: 1.5;
        color: var(--c-text);
        /* A horizontal ruler every line-height so any mismatch between the
           two columns' cap-height/baseline is visible at a glance, without
           needing to measure anything by hand. --c-rule-in is the "hairline
           between rows inside an already-opened group" token — a subtle,
           low-contrast line is exactly what a ruler background wants to be
           in both themes, not a hardcoded #eee that vanishes or glares
           depending on which theme is active. */
        background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(
          to bottom,
          var(--c-rule-in) 0,
          var(--c-rule-in) 1px,
          transparent 1px,
          transparent 36px
        );
      }
      .label {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 12px;
        color: var(--c-muted);
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
      }
      .note {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 12px;
        color: var(--c-muted);
        max-width: 60ch;
      }
      /* Column A — the REAL font stack: real file, then its matched
         fallback, then a generic. In a normal network load this is what
         every real component uses. */
      .real {
        font-family: var(--font-serif);
      }
      /* Column B — the fallback face FORCED, bypassing the real font file
         entirely. This isolates whether the metric-override math from 1.2
         is correct, independent of network timing. If the overrides are
         right, this column's text sits on the same ruler lines as column A
         even though the letterforms (Georgia vs Source Serif) look different. */
      .forced-fallback {
        /* stylelint-disable-next-line declaration-property-value-allowed-list
           -- deliberate exception: this test needs to isolate the FALLBACK
           face specifically, bypassing the real font entirely. var(--font-serif)
           can't express that — it always lists the real font first. No real
           component should ever do this; only this diagnostic page has a
           legitimate reason to. */
        font-family: 'Source Serif 4 Fallback', serif;
      }
      #theme-toggle {
        font: inherit;
        padding: 6px 10px;
        cursor: pointer;
        color: var(--c-text);
        background: var(--c-surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--c-rule);
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1 style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 16px;">
      Font metric check — compare row by row
    </h1>

    <p class="note">
      <button id="theme-toggle">toggle theme</button>
      &nbsp; effective theme: <strong id="theme-readout">?</strong>
      &nbsp; (hard-reload after choosing one, per the guidance below, to check for a flash)
    </p>

    <div class="row">
      <div>
        <div class="label">A — real stack (Source Serif 4 Var)</div>
        <p class="sample real">
          The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. Größe, Öffnung, Übung — testing
          German glyphs too. 1234567890.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="label">B — fallback FORCED (Georgia, overridden)</div>
        <p class="sample forced-fallback">
          The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. Größe, Öffnung, Übung — testing
          German glyphs too. 1234567890.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="note">
      If 1.2's math is right, both paragraphs' text sits on the same background ruler
      lines and looks roughly the same SIZE, despite different letterforms. A visible size
      jump or baseline drift between A and B means an override value is off — check
      size-adjust first, it has the largest effect.
    </p>

    <script>
      // This script's own timing does NOT need to be inline/synchronous —
      // it only wires up a button click and a readout, both of which happen
      // long after paint regardless. Only the theme-DECISION script above
      // has the before-paint constraint.
      //
      // Both getElementById calls are asserted non-null (`!`) rather than
      // null-checked defensively — this is a throwaway dev-only page with
      // fixed, known markup right above, not a component that needs to
      // tolerate a missing element.
      const readout = document.getElementById('theme-readout')!;
      const toggle = document.getElementById('theme-toggle')!;

      readout.textContent = window.__themeInit.currentEffectiveTheme();
      toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
        window.__themeInit.toggleTheme();
        readout.textContent = window.__themeInit.currentEffectiveTheme();
      });
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
